      "text": "I looked into Business Intelligence DNA and the entity behind businessinteldna.com. It appears to be a real, very new business, but there isn't yet enough independent history for me to describe it as well-established or independently validated.\n\nThe people behind it are Andrii (Andrey) Zhyla and Maryna (Masha) Zhyla. The site's own legal terms explicitly say that they personally conduct the diagnostic and strategy sessions: Andrii handles strategy/positioning and Maryna handles business economics and is described as a former CFO. ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/terms)) Maryna Zhyla also has an independently indexed LinkedIn profile identifying her with \"Wealthboosterpro Corp.\" and describing herself as an entrepreneur; the profile places her in the Los Angeles metropolitan area. ([linkedin.com](https://www.linkedin.com/in/marinazhyla?utm_source=openai))\n\nThe legal entity given on the site is Wealthboosterpro LLC, doing business as Business Intelligence DNA. It claims to be a Wyoming LLC and lists 5830 E 2nd St, Ste 7000, Casper, WY 82609. The website has unusually detailed terms, privacy disclosures, contact information, NDA provisions and pricing. Current listed offerings include a $500 \"Deep Diagnostics\" engagement and implementations starting at $5,000, along with AI phone-agent products. ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/terms))\n\nThere is an important qualification concerning that Wyoming address. It is not evidence of a substantial Business Intelligence DNA office in Casper. The exact address is used by Republic Registered Agent LLC and by numerous unrelated Wyoming companies, according to government filings. For example, Wyoming formation documents contained in SEC filings identify Republic Registered Agent LLC at exactly 5830 E 2nd St Ste 7000. ([sec.gov](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1945868/000194586823000006/exhibit_d_23.pdf?utm_source=openai)) In other words, this appears to be a registered-agent/formation address rather than the Zhylas' operating office. That's normal for small Wyoming LLCs, but it's worth knowing.\n\nThe other major caveat is age. The domain appears extraordinarily new. A domain-registration index lists businessinteldna.com among newly registered .com domains on September 6, 2026. ([com.all-url.info](https://com.all-url.info/21/25/?utm_source=openai)) The site's legal documents reinforce that timeline: its first privacy-policy version is dated September 8, 2026, and the current terms took effect September 14, 2026. ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/terms)) Today is September 18, so the public-facing Business Intelligence DNA brand appears to be only about two weeks old.\n\nI also found essentially no independent footprint for \"Business Intelligence DNA\" itself—no established review history, press coverage, substantial LinkedIn company presence, or independent client references surfaced in my searches. That doesn't demonstrate anything improper; for something launched this month, it is exactly what you might expect. But it means their claims and capabilities can't yet be corroborated through reputation.\n\nOne point in their favor is that the site is considerably more transparent than a typical questionable lead-generation site. It identifies the contracting entity and the two principals, publishes detailed contract terms, explains prices and limitations, names its technology/data processors (Stripe, Twilio, Anthropic, Google, ElevenLabs, etc.), explains where customer information goes, and explicitly says it does not guarantee business results. ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/terms)) Those are positive legitimacy signals, though they aren't proof of service quality.\n\nMy assessment:\n\n- Is there an identifiable business behind the website? Yes: Wealthboosterpro LLC, operated by Andrii and Maryna Zhyla. ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/terms))\n- Does it look like an obvious fake/scam website? I found no evidence that it is.\n- Is Business Intelligence DNA an established consulting firm with a verifiable track record? No evidence of that yet. It appears to have launched in September 2026.\n- Does the Wyoming address establish a real Casper office? No. It appears to be a commercial registered-agent address used by many companies. ([sec.gov](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1945868/000194586823000006/exhibit_d_23.pdf?utm_source=openai))\n- Would I treat a $500 engagement differently from a $5,000+ engagement? Yes. Given the lack of an independent track record, I would want verifiable client references and a clearly defined statement of work/deliverables before committing several thousand dollars.\n\nIf you're considering hiring them, I can go a level deeper and investigate Andrii and Maryna Zhyla themselves—previous businesses, corporate filings, professional history, reviews/complaints, lawsuits, and whether the \"ex-CFO\"/30-years-experience claims can be independently substantiated. That would give you a much stronger due-diligence answer than assessing the two-week-old website alone."
